1. POLICY CHECKPOINTS
-------------------------------------------------------------------------------

Thirty-four policies, and every checkpoint they load is third-party. Nineteen
weight files are committed beside the policy that loads them; ./download_weights.sh
pulls twenty-seven from their upstream repositories, so you take those under the
upstream's terms rather than under this repository's; ./export_onnx.py writes
ten on your machine -- six ONNX conversions, sonic's retyped planner, its
batched planner and encoder, and bfm_zero's latent table. Every checkpoint can now be obtained automatically,
though mturan33's needs a HuggingFace token whose account has accepted its
gate; wcompton's, the one exception until its upstream came back, is fetched
again.

Some rows are ONNX conversions, written by ./export_onnx.py from the TorchScript
checkpoints above them so that a policy can run on TensorRT. A conversion is a
derivative of its source and carries the source's terms unchanged -- putting a
weight in another container grants no right that did not already exist. So each
conversion ships only where its source's terms allow it to: amo's and rl_gym's
are committed because their checkpoints are redistributable, while robomimic's,
g1_gym's and stepdown's are generated on your machine because those checkpoints
declare no licence at all. mturan33's is written on your machine too, for a
different reason: its source is MIT and could be redistributed, but the gate on
the upstream repository is the author's to open, and shipping a conversion of a
gated file would route around a decision that is theirs to make. bfm_zero's latents.csv is the same reasoning in a
different container -- twelve vectors lifted out of a CC BY-NC pickle are still
CC BY-NC -- so it too is written on your machine rather than committed.
The amo and rl_gym checkpoints themselves are fetched rather than committed --
that is a repository-size choice about PyTorch blobs, not a licence one, and it
does not affect their conversions.

The sha256 column is the first four hex of the file's sha256 -- an identifier
for this table, not an integrity check. `sha256sum policies/*/model*` gives the
real thing. The committed files verify against this record as committed, and
./download_weights.sh carries the full sha256 of every file it fetches and
refuses a download that does not match it.

