Four flags worth reading twice:

- Ten policies load a checkpoint that declares no licence at all, which makes
  it effectively all-rights-reserved: dm_agile, dm_march, g1_gym, handoff, legged_rl_lab,
  mimic_lite, robomimic, run_residual, stepdown and wcompton. That is why none of them is
  committed here -- this repository will not hand you a copy it has no right to
  hand you. The Apache-2.0 LICENSE circulating alongside the RoboMimic weights
  belongs to AMO, a different project.

- homie and bfm_zero are CC BY-NC. Research and non-commercial use only; a
  commercial build must exclude both. They are fetched rather than committed,
  because an MIT repository cannot sublicense a non-commercial network. The
  same reasoning holds for bfm_zero's latents.csv, a 38 KB table derived from a
  CC BY-NC pickle, which is why ./export_onnx.py writes it on your machine
  instead of this repository shipping it.

- amo is Apache-2.0 but sim-only by its authors' explicit request. The upstream
  README warns against third-party hardware deployment, citing a sim-to-real
  failure that damaged a robot.

- sonic is NVIDIA's own model and is now fetched from NVIDIA. Until this
  revision the three files came from BlackCatRoboticsAI/g1-sonic-base, a
  third-party mirror whose card said "NVIDIA Open Model License (commercial
  use permitted with attribution)" while the link beside it pointed at NVIDIA's
  OneWay *Noncommercial* licence PDF -- a contradiction this file used to
  record and pass on to you. nvidia/GEAR-SONIC carries the same three files
  byte for byte, identical sha256, and states the terms plainly: Apache-2.0
  for the code, NVIDIA Open Model for the checkpoints, commercial use
  permitted with attribution. That is what the rows above record. The code
  those terms cover is NVlabs/GR00T-WholeBodyControl, which the model card
  is a deployment guide for; sonic releases no repository of its own, so it
  and gr00t_wbc share one upstream codebase and differ only in checkpoint.

  https://github.com/NVlabs/GR00T-WholeBodyControl

gr00t_wbc, the candidate that wins the campaign, is NVIDIA Open Model. If
licence terms decide your build, wbc_agile_velocity and wbc_agile
(Apache-2.0, both NVIDIA's WBC-AGILE) are the highest-placed permissively
licensed candidates, with wbc_agile_velocity finishing more of the tour.


2. UNITREE G1 ROBOT DESCRIPTION
-------------------------------------------------------------------------------

The Unitree G1 robot description — assets/g1_29dof.xml and all 36 meshes
under assets/meshes/ — comes from unitree_rl_gym, at
resources/robots/g1_description/, and is redistributed under the BSD 3-Clause
license reproduced below.

  https://github.com/unitreerobotics/unitree_rl_gym

The 36 STL meshes are Modified Versions: every one is decimated to at most a
thousand triangles and 50 KB, which takes the set from 22 MB to 1.7 MB. Twenty
seven of them are collision geometry, and MuJoCo collides a mesh by its convex
hull, so the decimation cuts the hulls from 34,643 vertices to 5,287 and a tour
from 7.8 s to 5.8 s of physics. It also changes contact: the hulls are close to
upstream's but not identical -- torso_link, the heaviest at 44,658 triangles,
loses about 6 mm across a 215 mm span -- so results measured here are not
comparable with results measured against the upstream meshes. Nothing but the
surfaces changed; the kinematics, inertials and joint limits are upstream's.

assets/g1_29dof.xml is a Modified Version: the simulation options and contact
defaults this benchmark runs with were added, joint armature, damping and
friction loss were filled in, and some collision geoms were made invisible.

